---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ArticleHero from '../../components/ArticleHero.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Haircuts Guide: Types, Face Shapes & Maintenance"
  description="A guide to common haircut types, how to choose one for your face shape and hair type, and how often to get trims."
  canonicalPath="/hair-services/haircuts/"
>
  <ArticleHero eyebrow="Hair Services" title="Haircuts: A Practical Guide" image="/images/gallery/gallery-mens-fade-collage.jpg" imageAlt="Finished fade haircut, front and side views" />
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
    <p class="text-xs text-ink/50 mb-2"><a href="/" class="hover:underline">Home</a> / <a href="/hair-services/" class="hover:underline">Hair Services</a> / Haircuts</p>

    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Of everything you can ask for at a salon, the haircut is the most personal —
      it's the one service that changes how you look every single day until your
      next appointment. That makes it worth understanding before you sit in the
      chair, whether you're seeing a stylist for the first time or trying to explain
      a change to someone new. This guide walks through the main families of
      haircuts, how face shape and hair type influence what actually works, and how
      to keep a cut looking sharp between visits.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-3">Common Types of Haircuts</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Haircuts generally fall into a few broad families: blunt cuts with a single,
      even length; layered cuts that remove weight and add movement; and textured or
      shag-style cuts built around choppy, irregular layers. Within each family,
      length and styling choices create endless variation. A blunt cut can be worn
      chin-length or waist-length; a layered cut can be subtle, with just a few
      face-framing pieces, or dramatic, with heavy layering throughout. Most
      stylists start a consultation by narrowing down which of these three families
      you're drawn to before talking specifics.
    </p>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      For shorter styles, the same logic applies at a smaller scale. A crop, a bob,
      or a pixie can each be cut blunt for a graphic, sharp line, or layered for
      softness and volume. Barbering-style cuts add another dimension — fades and
      tapers that graduate length gradually from the neckline up, which is its own
      skill separate from scissor cutting and usually calls for a barber or a
      stylist who specifically works with clippers.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-3">Choosing a Haircut for Your Face Shape</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      As a general rule, cuts with volume near the jaw tend to soften angular faces,
      while cuts that add height at the crown can balance a rounder face. Longer,
      face-framing layers can draw the eye downward and lengthen a shorter or wider
      face, while blunt, chin-length shapes tend to widen the lower half of the
      face — useful if you want to soften a very narrow jawline, less useful if your
      face is already round. These are starting points, not rules — bring reference
      photos to your consultation so your stylist can adapt them to your specific
      features rather than a generic chart.
    </p>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      It also helps to think about proportion beyond just the face itself. Bangs,
      parting, and length all interact with your forehead height, brow shape and
      even glasses frames. A style that reads as flattering in a photo of someone
      else may sit completely differently on you once those variables change — which
      is exactly why an in-person consultation, not just a picture, gets the best
      result.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-3">Choosing a Haircut for Your Hair Type</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Fine, straight hair often benefits from blunt lines that create the appearance
      of thickness, since too many thin layers can make already-fine hair look even
      sparser at the ends. Thick, straight hair usually has the opposite problem —
      it can look heavy or triangular without enough layering to remove bulk and let
      it move.
    </p>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Curly or coily hair usually looks best cut dry, with layers shaped to work
      with the hair's natural curl pattern rather than against it, since curls
      shrink upward significantly once they dry and a cut done wet can end up much
      shorter — or much less balanced — than expected. If you have curly hair, it's
      worth specifically asking whether your stylist regularly cuts curls dry;
      it's a different technique from a standard wet cut, and experience matters.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-3">How Often to Get a Trim</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Most people do well with a trim every 6–8 weeks to maintain shape and manage
      split ends, though hair that's growing out or being kept very short — like a
      fade or a cropped pixie — may need trims every 3–4 weeks to hold its line.
      Longer styles that are simply growing out with minimal shape can often stretch
      to 10–12 weeks between visits. If you're not sure where you fall, ask your
      stylist to recommend a schedule based on how quickly your particular cut loses
      its shape, since that varies more by style than by hair type alone.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-3">Getting the Most Out of a Consultation</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      A good consultation is a conversation, not a monologue — the more specific you
      can be about what you liked and didn't like about past cuts, how much time you
      spend styling day to day, and what you actually want to maintain at home, the
      easier it is for a stylist to translate a reference photo into something that
      will genuinely work for your hair and your routine.
    </p>

    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      See also: <a href="/hairstyles/by-face-shape/" class="text-rose-600 underline">Hairstyles by Face Shape</a>,
      <a href="/tools/hair-length-calculator/" class="text-rose-600 underline">Hair Length Calculator</a>,
      <a href="/tools/salon-cost-calculator/" class="text-rose-600 underline">Salon Cost Calculator</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
